The implementation model does not perform independent review itself. Items 2, 4, and the independent-review portion of 5 are satisfied only by reviewer-authored evidence on the review branch / ledger process. The runner’s job is to notice those records and re-evaluate which implementation item is now actionable.

## 16.2 Legacy P0/P1 reconsideration gate

Before continuing deep into lower-priority UI issues, perform an **implementation-side fresh current-source revalidation/self-audit** of the still-open historical P0/P1 defects from Section 10, especially:

- `BUG-OBSERVE-01`;
- `BUG-BACKUP-03`;
- `BUG-BACKUP-04`;
- `BUG-KEYWORD-01`;
- `BUG-METADATA-01`.

This revalidation is not an independent CLEAN review and does not require a reviewer record before the runner continues. Its purpose is to determine whether current source still contains an actionable implementation defect, whether a dependency/evidence gate applies, or whether intervening code already satisfies/removes the historical path.

If current source confirms an open destructive P0, it takes priority over lower-severity planned items even if the historical ordinal was later. If current source already satisfies the invariant, record `NO_CHANGE_REQUIRED_CURRENT_HEAD`; if the historical path no longer exists and no equivalent reachable path remains, record `NOT_APPLICABLE_CURRENT_HEAD`. Neither state establishes independent CLEAN, and neither is a reason to idle.

The implementation model should use severity + hard dependency + current source evidence, not blindly numeric order.

## 16.3 Dependency-driven legacy sequence

Preserve these hard relationships:

```text
BUG-OBSERVE-01
    └── BUG-KEYWORD-01

BUG-METADATA-02
    └── BUG-METADATA-01

BUG-DATE-01
    └── BUG-DATE-02

BUG-HISTORY-01
    └── BUG-KEYWORD-02

BUG-BACKUP-04
    ├── BUG-BACKUP-05
    └── BUG-BACKUP-07

BUG-BACKUP-03
    requires applicable backup/restore prerequisites
    including completed BUG-BACKUP-01 semantic authorization
```

Where multiple independent defects are actionable, prefer destructive/data-integrity P0/P1 over cosmetic/UI P2/P3.

---

# 17. Final Known-Good Baseline gate

Do not create a Known-Good Baseline merely because the implementation model exhausted the queue it managed to reach.

A final baseline requires:

Waiver policy before evaluating the checklist:

- **P0:** no baseline waiver.
- **P1:** normally blocks Known-Good Baseline. Any exception requires an explicit project-owner decision plus independently documented risk; it must never be inferred from schedule/resource pressure.
- **P2:** may remain only through an explicit project-owner waiver recorded by independent review.
- **P3/non-blocker:** does not block unless explicitly elevated.

Then:

1. all agreed remediation-scope P0/P1/P2 defects independently closed or explicitly waived under the policy above;
2. all closure records sealed;
3. no unresolved review inbox blocker;
4. full JVM suite where practical;
5. Android-test compile;
6. full Kotlin compile;
7. `assembleDebug`;
8. representative Room/WorkManager/filesystem/SAF/player/restore smoke coverage where applicable;
9. migration tests if final backup/reset design adds migrations;
10. final independent high-capability review over the agreed complete remediation range;
11. remediation of final-review blockers;
12. authoritative ledger update;
13. only then a Known-Good Baseline commit/tag under project policy.

Do not let unrelated feature work outrun unresolved correctness findings inside the agreed scope.

---

# 18. Token auto-extractor feature boundary

This feature begins only after the download-related correctness gate chosen by the project owner is complete.

When that time arrives, start from fresh current source and map:

```text
existing manual token entry
    ↓
validation
    ↓
storage format
    ↓
player-client association
    ↓
request construction
    ↓
update/expiry behavior
```

Then design:

```text
automatic extraction
    ↓
validation and source attribution
    ↓
safe persistence/update
    ↓
manual fallback
    ↓
clear user-visible failure behavior
```

Do not reuse correctness-remediation commits to introduce this feature. It receives its own feature design, tests, and review scope.

---

# 19. Master-plan revision policy

The authoritative versioned copy of this file lives on `plan/remediation`. Its canonical text is the lexical-order concatenation of `remediation/master-plan/parts/*.part`, with `remediation/master-plan/README.md` as the manifest. Part boundaries have no governance meaning. Plan history is additive and separate from implementation, independent review, and closure history.

This file is intentionally detailed, but it is not allowed to override fresh code reality.

Revise the plan only when concrete evidence warrants it, for example:

- current implementation already eliminated a planned root cause;
- a hard dependency proves false;
- a fix introduces a new same-domain blocker;
- repository history establishes a legacy backup contract that was previously unknown;
- product owner records a quality/fallback semantic decision;
- independent review reclassifies or splits a defect;
- two historically separate defects are proven to share one exact state machine and correction;
- a current architecture makes a proposed persistence mechanism unnecessary/incorrect.

When revising:

- commit the revision additively on `plan/remediation`;
- never amend/rebase/squash/force-push referenced plan history;
- preserve defect IDs and historical relation where possible;
- distinguish historical observation from current source;
- do not retroactively rewrite closure evidence;
- do not turn a planning edit into an independent-review decision;
- do not expect an already-running implementation agent to silently adopt the new Plan SHA; a new Plan SHA governs a run only when it is resolved at bootstrap or explicitly adopted by user instruction.

---

# 20. Implementation handoff report template

After each candidate defect push, report at least:

```text
Defect:
Pinned Plan SHA:
Starting implementation HEAD:
Starting review HEAD:
Starting ledger HEAD:
Reviewed external records:

Current-source root cause confirmed/revised:
Invariant implemented:

Changed files:
Semantic changes:
Preserved behaviors:

Self-review findings:
Additional same-domain fixes:

Verification:
  git diff --check:
  focused JVM:
  Kotlin compile:
  androidTest compile:
  instrumentation:
  regressions:
  Samsung SM-A546E: NOT EXECUTED — DEVICE UNAVAILABLE

Initial failures and reruns:

Commit SHA:
Commit message:
Parent:
Final remote implementation HEAD:
Final remote review HEAD:
Final remote ledger HEAD:

Independent-review status: PENDING
Do not claim CLEAN.
```

For a multi-defect Ultra run, repeat this block per defect/correction chain and finish with a queue summary:

- candidates pushed;
- review-fixes pushed;
- defects blocked by evidence/product decision;
- next actionable defect;
- working tree status;
- remote branch heads.

---

# 21. Final operational summary

The complete remediation workflow is:

```text
THIS MASTER PLAN
    ↓
implementation model fresh-fetches implementation + review + ledger
    ↓
respond to any higher-priority independent review blocker
    ↓
select highest-priority ACTIONABLE defect
    ↓
fix one correction domain
    ↓
focused tests + production-boundary evidence
    ↓
fresh self-review of the whole defect domain
    ↓
additive commit + push
    ↓
fresh-fetch review/remediation
    ↓
review blocker now available? ── yes ──> additive review-fix + tests + push
      │
      no
      ↓
new CLEAN record unlocks dependency? ── yes ──> re-prioritize unlocked defect/gate
      │
      no
      ↓
move to next unrelated ACTIONABLE defect as capacity permits
    ↓
independent reviewer reviews exact candidates on review/remediation
    ↓
NOT CLEAN ──> implementation runner later creates additive review-fix
CLEAN ──────> dependency may unlock; metadata-only ledger closure when required
    ↓
Finding B CLEAN
    ↓
mandatory full current-source F1 close-out + ledger closure
    ↓
all agreed defects closed
    ↓
final heavy verification / full-range independent review
    ↓
Known-Good Baseline
    ↓
separate token auto-extractor feature work
```

The implementation model's objective is **maximum safe corrected code**, not maximum number of touched defect IDs. A smaller number of genuinely coherent, tested candidates is preferable to broad partially fixed changes. Conversely, once a correction is coherent and pushed, the model should continue to another **actionable** defect rather than stopping unnecessarily. Pending review never upgrades a candidate to CLEAN, and dependency-gated defects remain blocked until reviewer-authored evidence opens the gate. When a planned item is blocked or already satisfied in current source, apply the Global No-Idle Rule in Section 11.2.1 and rescan the complete registry instead of waiting.


# Appendix A. Defect registry at plan generation

This table is a navigation index, not a substitute for the dossiers. `Historical severity` is carried from the prior master plan where available; newer entries use the most recent project-review priority. Fresh current evidence may reclassify severity. A legacy item that current implementation-side revalidation finds already satisfied may transition to `NO_CHANGE_REQUIRED_CURRENT_HEAD`; one whose historical reachable path no longer exists may transition to `NOT_APPLICABLE_CURRENT_HEAD`. These are runner states, not independent closure verdicts, and the runner must continue scanning other work.

| Defect / work item | Historical/current priority | State at generation | Source confidence | Primary dossier |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Finding A / BUG-BACKUP-01-A | previously blocking F1 | independently CLEAN, ledger CLOSED | independently established | 0.2 |
| BUG-OUTPUT-01 | current known P2 blocker inside historically P0 output domain | NOT CLEAN | confirmed current through latest independent review | 9.1 |
| Finding B / BUG-BACKUP-01-B | destructive authorization follow-up | BLOCKED_BY_DEPENDENCY on BUG-OUTPUT-01 independent CLEAN | confirmed contract from prior independent review | 9.2 |
| Player timeline/index crash | P1 | pending current-source implementation | review-history grounded | 9.3 |
| Crash evidence preservation | high diagnostic prerequisite | pending | requires fresh source confirmation | 9.4 |
| MainActivity startup/lifecycle crash | severity depends on causal evidence | BLOCKED_BY_EVIDENCE until causal evidence is sufficient; skip and continue unrelated work | root cause unknown | 9.5 |
| Link input/share handling | current priority | pending | review-history grounded | 9.6 |
| Canonical media/source identity | current priority / destructive dependency | pending | review-history grounded | 9.7 |
